{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And he strewed.</b> A term of scattering — and so ״יְזֹרֶה עַל נָוֵהוּ גָפְרִית״ (Job 18), and so ״כִּי חִנָּם מְזֹרָה הָרֶשֶׁת״ (Proverbs 1) — with which one winnows grain and legumes." "<b>And he made the children of Israel drink.</b> He intended to test them like a suspected wife [sotah]; three modes of death were judged there: if there were witnesses and warning — by the sword, like the law of the men of a seduced city, who are many; witnesses without warning — by plague, as it says ״וַיִּגֹּף YHWH אֶת הָעָם״; no witnesses and no warning — by the waters that test [mei ha-idrot], which examined them through the water and their bellies swelled (Yoma 66)."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {1 "Rashi writes הִדְרוֹקָן (ha-drāqan), which is his term for the testing-waters of the sotah; the text of Yoma 66b to which he refers discusses the three modes of death at Sinai."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:56:15.692978288Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}